"Distill Until Divergence:Error-Truncated Hindsight Distillation for Agentic RL (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23048v1)\nciteturn41226view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23048v1\",\"lineno\":350}); Total lines: 412\nL232: Complete truncation achieves the highest success rate and score in both sweeps (Table cite81†4 ). Reducing $\\alpha$ from 1 to 0.25 raises WebShop success from 77.6% to 79.0% for Positive suffix guidance and from 72.2% to 76.6% for Negative guidance. Both trail ETRO’s 80.8% by 1.8 and 4.2 points. Both variants also remain below ETRO in task score. Thus, weakening suffix supervision improves both variants relative to full-strength supervision, but none of the tested nonzero weights matches complete removal.\nL233: Table 4: Post-error OPD weight sweep on WebShop using Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct, with $\\lambda=0.001$.\nL234: Method  | $\\alpha$  | Success (%)  | Score\nL235: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL236: ETRO  | 0  | 80.8  | 87.9\nL237: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL238: Suffix Positive  | 0.25  | 79.0  | 85.1\nL239: Suffix Positive  | 0.5  | 78.4  | 85.2\nL240: Suffix Positive  | 1  | 77.6  | 84.6\nL241: Suffix Negative  | 0.25  | 76.6  | 82.7\nL242: Suffix Negative  | 0.5  | 74.4  | 80.6\nL243: Suffix Negative  | 1  | 72.2  | 79.3\nL244: ### 4.4 Analysis Experiments\nL245: \nL246: Training behavior. To examine whether ETRO’s final gains are accompanied by sustained improvement during optimization, we compare GRPO, Global Positive, Global Negative, and ETRO using Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct on WebShop. We track unsmoothed training reward, validation success, and policy entropy at the same recorded checkpoints (Figure cite82†3 ), assessing task performance alongside changes in policy concentration.\nL247: ETRO maintains the highest validation success at every recorded checkpoint without a monotonic decrease in policy entropy. Its success rises from 47.8% at step 20 to 80.8% at step 160, exceeding the final Global Positive, Global Negative, and GRPO results by 3.6, 12.0, and 18.8 percentage points, respectively. Training reward reaches 0.9531 at step 120 and fluctuates afterward, while validation success continues to improve.\nL248: Over the same late-stage interval, ETRO’s entropy recovers from 0.351 to 0.804, whereas Global Negative finishes at 0.363 with lower success. These observations show that the reported performance gains coexist with sustained validation improvement and a recovery in response diversity, rather than uniformly increasing policy concentration.\nL249: Negative-skill placement. To examine whether failure-specific guidance benefits from placement at the decision it describes, we vary the location of the one-turn Negative OPD mask using Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct on WebShop. For failed trajectories with nonempty prefixes and suffixes, the writer produces a Negative skill for the validated error at turn $k$.\nL250: Holding that skill’s content fixed, we apply the mask either at $k$, at a randomly sampled turn from the pre-error prefix, or at a randomly sampled turn from the post-error suffix. Only the location of Negative supervision varies; the supervised turn count remains fixed.\nL251: Applying Negative guidance at the diagnosed error achieves the highest success and score among the three tested placements (Figure cite83†4 ). Error-turn conditioning reaches 80.8% success and an 87.9 score, compared with 75.4% and 84.3 for prefix placement and 71.8% and 81.5 for suffix placement. The corresponding success gains are 5.4 and 9.0 percentage points, with score gains of 3.6 and 6.4.\nL252: These results favor localizing the correction signal at the diagnosed decision rather than shifting it to an earlier or later turn.\nL253: Writer reliability. To assess the quality of the skills and error boundaries that support routing, we evaluate 100 failed WebShop trajectories sampled after Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct training. Five annotators each inspect 20 distinct complete trajectories to establish reference annotations. Using these human annotations as reference answers, GPT-5.6 sol evaluates the writer outputs for boundary acceptance, exact-turn agreement, workflow validity, and correction validity. Results appear in Table cite84†5 .\nL254: The reference-based evaluation supports the quality of the writer-generated skills and error boundaries on the sampled WebShop trajectories. Boundary acceptance reaches 92.0%, with 84.0% exact-turn agreement against human references; workflow and correction validity reach 92.0% and 90.0%, respectively.\nL255: These results support the writer’s ability to identify relevant error boundaries and produce actionable guidance for both OPD branches, providing empirical support for the skill generation and localization underlying ETRO’s routing.\nL256: Table 5: Writer quality on WebShop, evaluated against human references by GPT-5.6 sol (%).\nL257: Metric  | Rate (%)\nL258: --- | ---\nL259: Boundary acceptance  | 92.0\nL260: Exact-turn agreement  | 84.0\nL261: Workflow validity  | 92.0\nL262: Correction validity  | 90.0\nL263: Figure 4: Comparison of Negative-skill conditioning regions on WebShop using Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct.\nL264: Post-error recovery. To assess whether suffix truncation improves behavior after a recoverable error, we compare actors from the same perturbed states on held-out WebShop tasks, collecting repeated continuations without hindsight skills. The actors retain Positive prefix and Negative error-turn guidance during training but differ in suffix treatment: Suffix Positive and Suffix Negative continue the corresponding OPD signal, while ETRO retains only GRPO.\nL265: We report recovery success, normalized score, wrong-branch continuation, and first-turn action entropy; the controlled-error construction and evaluation procedure appear in Appendix cite49†C.2 .\nL266: ETRO achieves the highest recovery success and score and the lowest wrong-branch continuation rate among the compared actors (Table cite85†6 ). It reaches 63.8% success and an 82.6 score, compared with 54.6% and 74.8 for Suffix Positive and 49.2% and 69.7 for Suffix Negative. Wrong-branch continuation falls to 23.7%, versus 33.9% and 40.6%, reductions of 10.2 and 16.9 percentage points. ETRO’s first-turn entropy is 0.62, compared with 0.55 and 0.39 for the suffix variants.\nL267: These results indicate that removing post-error skill supervision while retaining GRPO helps agents recover from errors rather than persist along an incorrect action branch. Without hindsight skills at evaluation, these differences reflect learned behavior. Higher entropy accompanies better recovery and fewer wrong-branch continuations, rather than action variability alone.\nL268: Table 6: Post-error recovery on WebShop.\nL269: Method  | Recovery  | Recovery  | Wrong  | Entropy\nL270: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL271: Success  | Score  | Branch\nL272: --- | --- | ---\nL273: Suffix Positive  | 54.6  | 74.8  | 33.9  | 0.55\nL274: Suffix Negative  | 49.2  | 69.7  | 40.6  | 0.39\nL275: ETRO  | 63.8  | 82.6  | 23.7  | 0.62\nL276: ## 5 Conclusion\nL277: We presented ETRO to address the mismatch between hindsight supervision and evolving interaction states. By separating workflow guidance from local error correction, ETRO routes OPD by skill polarity and trajectory position while preserving GRPO throughout the rollout. Experiments on WebShop, ALFWorld, and Search QA demonstrate competitive performance across model scales.\nL278: Ablations support complementary skill routing and post-error truncation, while recovery experiments show improved recovery and less wrong-branch continuation. These findings suggest that effective hindsight credit allocation depends on both skill content and its temporal applicability. Future work could extend routing to multiple error and recovery boundaries.\nL279: ## Limitations\nL280: \nL281: ETRO depends on the writer to locate errors and generate hindsight skills. Our audit and ablation studies support the effectiveness of this design, but its performance may vary with the quality of the writer’s outputs.\nL282: \nL283: Finally, hindsight generation and policy re-scoring add training-time computation even though skills are absent at inference time. The approach depends on access to complete trajectories and outcome feedback, which may be costly or unavailable in some applications.\nL284: ## Ethical Considerations\nL285: \nL286: Training language-model agents to act more effectively can also increase the consequences of unsafe actions. Success in benchmark environments does not establish safety for deployment with real purchases, private information, or unrestricted external tools. Such deployment would require additional safeguards, authorization checks, and task-specific evaluation beyond the scope of this work.\nL287: Interaction trajectories and hindsight skills can contain user requests, retrieved text, or other sensitive information. Any release or reuse of these artifacts should respect applicable dataset and model licenses, protect personal information, and preserve the anonymity of review materials. Writer-generated diagnoses are fallible and should not be treated as authoritative judgments of users or annotators.\nL288: ## AI Assistance Disclosure\nL289: \nL290: An AI assistant helped edit manuscript wording and formatting, including initial drafts of the Limitations and Ethical Considerations sections. The authors are responsible for verifying the final text, references, and reported results.\nL291: ## References\nL292:   * Ahmadian et al. (2024) A. Ahmadian, C. Cremer, M. Gallé, M. Fadaee, J. Kreutzer, O. Pietquin, A. Üstün, and S. Hooker Back to basics: revisiting reinforce-style optimization for learning from human feedback in llms. In Proceedings of the 62nd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (Volume 1: Long Papers), pp. 12248–12267. Cited by: cite86†§2 .\nL2