ds in Natural Language Processing, pp. 7920–7939. Cited by: cite88†§1 , cite86†§2 .\nL309:   * Wu et al. (2026) J. Wu, S. Yang, Z. Lu, F. Zhang, Y. Shen, L. Feng, H. Luo, Z. Lian, S. Zhang, Z. Wen, et al. SEED: self-evolving on-policy distillation for agentic reinforcement learning. arXiv preprint arXiv:2607.14777. Cited by: cite91†§B.1 , cite94†§1 , cite89†§2 , cite92†§4.1 .\nL310:   * Yang et al. (2026a) C. Yang, C. Qin, Q. Si, M. Chen, N. Gu, D. Yao, Z. Lin, W. Wang, J. Wang, and N. Duan Self-distilled rlvr. arXiv preprint arXiv:2604.03128. Cited by: cite89†§2 .\nL311:   * Yang et al. (2026b) S. Yang, J. Wu, Z. Lu, Y. Shen, F. Zhang, L. Feng, S. Zhang, H. Luo, Z. Lian, Z. Wen, et al. Opid: on-policy skill distillation for agentic reinforcement learning. arXiv preprint arXiv:2606.26790. Cited by: cite91†§B.1 , cite94†§1 , cite89†§2 , cite92†§4.1 .\nL312:   * Yao et al. (2022) S. Yao, H. Chen, J. Yang, and K. Narasimhan Webshop: towards scalable real-world web interaction with grounded language agents. Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems 35, pp. 20744–20757. Cited by: cite95†§B.1 , cite96†§4.1 .\nL313:   * Yu et al. (2026) Q. Yu, Z. Zhang, R. Zhu, Y. Yuan, X. Zuo, Y. Yue, W. Dai, T. Fan, G. Liu, L. Liu, et al. Dapo: an open-source llm reinforcement learning system at scale. Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems 38, pp. 113222–113244. Cited by: cite86†§2 .\nL314:   * Zhao et al. (2026a) S. Zhao, Z. Xie, M. Liu, J. Huang, G. Pang, F. Chen, and A. Grover Self-distilled reasoner: on-policy self-distillation for large language models. arXiv preprint arXiv:2601.18734. Cited by: cite89†§2 .\nL315:   * Zhao et al. (2026b) Z. Zhao, X. Ma, L. Yang, Y. Feng, D. Shi, J. He, X. Xin, Z. Ren, and X. Wu Rosd: reflective on-policy self-distillation for language model reasoning across domains. arXiv preprint arXiv:2605.28014. Cited by: cite93†§2 .\nL316:   * Zheng et al. (2026) H. Zheng, Y. Zhu, Q. Wang, and W. Zhang Trajectory-relative hindsight distillation for agentic reinforcement learning. arXiv preprint arXiv:2608.07371. Cited by: cite89†§2 .\nL317: ## Appendix A Writer Prompt and Output Format\nL318: The frozen writer receives the task, complete trajectory, episode outcome, and candidate step indices. Its JSON output contains episode_summary, episode_skill, and step_skills. The global episode_skill supplies Positive guidance, while step_skills provides a local Negative correction for the first unrecovered decisive error in a failed episode, with at most one entry. The prompt uses zero-based step indices; the corresponding one-based turn index in the method is obtained by adding one.\nL319: #### Boundary selection and recovery.\nL320: The writer evaluates an error using the complete later trajectory, rather than selecting the first locally invalid action. An error is recovered when subsequent actions correct its consequences, as supported by later observations and task progress; such an error is excluded from boundary selection even if the episode ultimately fails for another reason. Among multiple errors, the writer selects the earliest candidate that remains unrecovered and is judged decisive for the observed failure.\nL321: A format violation alone is insufficient: it must pass the same recovery and decisiveness tests. If no such error can be identified confidently, the writer returns an empty step_skills object and the failed trajectory retains Positive OPD throughout alongside GRPO. This is a diagnosis of the realized episode, not a claim that the selected action makes failure inevitable.\nL322: #### Candidate indices.\nL323: \nL324: The candidate list contains all interaction steps in the complete trajectory: for a trajectory with $T$ turns, the zero-based list is $\\{0,\\ldots,T-1\\}$. No recorded interaction steps are filtered out before boundary selection, including steps with format-invalid responses. The writer selects only from this complete list, so “first” means earliest in the complete trajectory satisfying the unrecovered-decisive-error definition, not earliest within a filtered subset.\nL325: Analyze the following agent episode and return ONLY valid JSON. This experiment routes hindsight according to its temporal scope: - episode_skill is global positive guidance and must always describe what the policy should do to complete the task, regardless of whether the sampled episode succeeded or failed. - step_skills is local negative correction. It is used only for the first unrecovered decisive error in a failed episode and must include a concrete better decision, not merely a prohibition.\nL326: {{ROUTING_INSTRUCTION}} How to interpret action validity: - ‘Action valid‘ is a deterministic FORMAT signal produced by the WebShop action parser, not a judgment made by this analyzer. - ‘Action valid: false‘ means the response violated the required response format: it was missing a complete <think>...</think> block, missing a complete <action>...</action> block, or contained Chinese characters. Treat this as direct evidence of a format error at that step.\nL327: - ‘Action valid: true‘ means only that those format checks passed. It does NOT prove that the extracted action was admissible, executable, relevant, or semantically correct. Determine those properties from the current observation, admissible actions, and the following environment observation. - A format error is not automatically the decisive cause of episode failure. Apply the same recovery and decisiveness tests above using the complete later trajectory. Important constraints: - Step indexing is 0-based.\nL328: - Select only from the supplied candidate step indices. - The candidate step indices include every interaction step in the complete trajectory; no steps are filtered out. - Ground every statement in the task, observations, admissible actions, and complete trajectory. - Keep episode_skill concise, actionable, and useful at every decision point. - Express reusable decision rules rather than leaking literal product IDs or other future-only details that were not available at the decision point.\nL329: - Use at most one step_skills entry. A failed episode may use an empty object when no unrecovered decisive error can be identified confidently. - Return only: episode_summary, episode_skill, step_skills.\nL330: Return format: {  \"episode_summary\": \"concise factual summary\",  \"episode_skill\": \"global positive workflow\",  \"step_skills\": {{STEP_SKILLS_EXAMPLE}} } Episode context: - Task description: {{TASK_DESCRIPTION}} - episode_success: {{EPISODE_SUCCESS}} - Candidate step indices: {{CANDIDATE_STEP_INDICES}} - Interaction trajectory: {{INTERACTION_TRAJECTORY}}\nL331: Figure 5: WebShop writer prompt template.\nL332: ## Appendix B Implementation and Evaluation Details\nL333: \nL334: ### B.1 Benchmarks and Result Sources\nL335: #### ALFWorld and WebShop.\nL336: \nL337: ALFWorld (cite52†Shridhar et al., 2020 ) evaluates multi-step household tasks through textual observations and actions. We use 2,400 training examples from its training split and evaluate success on all 134 Unseen tasks. WebShop (cite78†Yao et al., 2022 ) requires agents to search, filter, configure, and purchase products under compositional constraints. We use 2,400 training examples and all 500 evaluation tasks, reporting normalized task score and exact success rate.\nL338: #### Search QA.\nL339: \nL340: We follow the Search-R1 training and evaluation protocol (cite63†Jin et al., 2025 ), using 19,200 training examples and evaluation on Natural Questions, TriviaQA, PopQA, HotpotQA, 2WikiMultiHopQA, MuSiQue, and Bamboogle. We report each dataset’s accuracy and their unweighted macro average.\nL341: #### Comparison methods and sources.\nL342: Table cite79†1 compares ETRO with GRPO, GRPO+OPSD, Skill-SD, RLSD, SDAR, OPID, SEED, and ICSD. All ETRO rows are from our local experiments; every non-ETRO row is an externally reported reference result drawn from the ICSD, OPID, and SEED reports (cite69†Lan et al., 2026 ; cite55†Yang et al., 2026b ; cite56†Wu et al., 2026 ), including baseline methods reported within those studies.\nL343: The local training-dynamics comparison (Figure cite82†3 ) and the polarity and truncation ablations (Tables cite80†3 and cite80†3 ) use Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct.\nL344: ### B.2 Training Configuration\nL345: \nL346: #### Actor and writer.\nL347: \nL348: We train Qwen2.5-Instruct actors at 1.5B, 3B, and 7B scales, using a frozen Qwen3.5-27B writer for all scales. After each completed episode, the writer generates hindsight skills, and the frozen behavior policy re-scores the original responses under the applicable skill contexts using teacher forcing. These operations add training-time computation; skills and writer calls are absent during evaluation.\nL349: #### Rollout batches and sequence limits.\nL350: Each training batch contains 16 tasks for ALFWorld and WebShop or 128 tasks for Search QA, with eight rollouts per task. Thus, an ALFWorld or WebShop batch contains 128 training trajectories. Maximum actor prompt lengths are 2,048 tokens for ALFWorld and 4,096 for WebShop and Search QA, with at most 512 response tokens per turn. Both training and evaluation allow up to 15 interaction turns for ALFWorld and WebShop and four for Search QA.\nL351: Overlong WebShop prompts are rejected, while Search QA uses left truncation.\nL352: #### Writer decoding.\nL353: \nL354: The writer uses temperature 0.6, top-$p=0.95$, and top-$k=20$, with a maximum output length of 4,096 tokens and a maximum context length of 65,536 tokens. Thinking is disabled.\nL355: #### Optimization and actor decoding.\nL356: \nL357: The actor uses AdamW with learning rate $10^{-6}$, betas $(0.9,0.999)$, and weight decay 0.01. We use one PPO epoch per collected batch, a clipping parameter of 0.2, and a KL-loss coefficient of 0.01. The WebShop PPO minibatch size is 64, with a per-GPU microbatch size of eight. Actor rollout decoding uses temperature 1.0 and top-$p=1$.\nL358: #### OPD weighting and loss aggregation.\nL359: ETRO adds the routed OPD token advantages to the GRPO advantages with a shared coefficient $\\lambda=0.001$ for both skill polarities, rather than introducing a separate cross-entropy loss. We use token-mean aggregation over all valid actor response tokens, excluding environment observations and padding. Tokens without OPD supervision remain in the denominator and retain their GRPO advantages.\nL360: The suffix-weight comparison uses this same denominator for every $\\alpha$, without separately normalizing by OPD-active or supervised suffix tokens.\nL361: ### B.3 Evaluation Protocol\nL362: \nL363: #### WebShop decoding and checkpoint selection.\nL364: \nL365: WebShop validation uses sampled decoding at temperature 0.4. The local 1.5B training-dynamics comparison runs for 160 optimization steps and reports each method’s final checkpoint rather than its best intermediate result; ETRO reaches 80.8% success at step 160.\nL366: #### Search QA evaluation.\nL367: \nL368: We use intfloat/e5-base-v2 and a FAISS flat index over wiki-18, retrieving three passages per query. Exact-match evaluation extracts the last <answer> span, lowercases it, removes punctuation and English articles, and collapses whitespace before comparison with accepted answers.\nL369: \nL370: ## Appendix C Additional Analysis Experiments\nL371: \nL372: ### C.1 Regional Alignment Diagnostic\nL373: #### Rollout collection and sample composition.\nL374: For each task prompt $x$, the frozen GRPO-trained actor samples eight complete trajectories without hindsight skills. These are eight rollouts from the task’s initial state, not eight continuations from each recorded turn. Both successful and failed trajectories contribute to return estimation. Only turns from failed trajectories with a valid writer-estimated first unrecovered decisive error $k_{i}$ enter the regional correlations in Figure cite76†1 ; successful turns are not relabeled as pre-error turns.\nL375: The 100-trajectory writer audit in Section cite24†4.4 is a separate analysis, not the stated sample size of this diagnostic.\nL376: #### Outcome returns and action pooling.\nL377: The terminal outcome is $R_{i}\\in\\{0,1\\}$, with success scored as one and failure as zero. With no intermediate outcome reward, an occurrence’s discounted future return is $G_{i,t}=\\gamma^{T_{i}-t}R_{i}$, where $\\gamma\\in[0,1]$ is the discount factor. Let $\\mathcal{H}_{x}(o)$ contain all turn occurrences $(i,t)$ among the eight rollouts whose pre-action anchor observation is exactly $o$. Its subset $\\mathcal{H}_{x}(o,a)$ contains occurrences executing the same action $a$.\nL378: Grouping is within a prompt, uses the executable action rather than the full reasoning response, and pools occurrences regardless of their trajectories’ final outcomes or error regions. We estimate an action’s return by\nL379:  | $$\\widehat{Q}_{x}(o,a)=\\frac{\\sum_{(i,t)\\in\\mathcal{H}_{x}(o,a)}G_{i,t}}{|\\mathcal{H}_{x}(o,a)|}.$$  |  | (4)\nL380: \nL381: The anchor baseline is the occurrence-weighted mean\nL382: \nL383:  | $$\\overline{G}_{x}(o)=\\frac{\\sum_{(i,t)\\in\\mathcal{H}_{x}(o)}G_{i,t}}{|\\mathcal{H}_{x}(o)|}.$$  |  | (5)\nL384: \nL385: Each retained occurrence receives the action-pooled reference advantage by subtracting the shared-anchor baseline from the pooled action return, without standard-deviation normalization:\nL386:  | $$A^{\\mathrm{ref}}_{i,t}=\\widehat{Q}_{x}(o_{i,t},a_{i,t})-\\overline{G}_{x}(o_{i,t}).$$  |  | (6)\nL387: \nL388: This GiGPO-style diagnostic reference is used only for the alignment analysis; ETRO is trained with the advantage in Equation cite97†3 .\nL389: #### Filtering and OPD comparison.\nL390: We discard singleton anchor groups and groups without distinct executable actions. The remaining occurrences contribute to the pooled reference; the plotted subset additionally requires a failed trajectory with a valid $k_{i}$. For each such turn, we average Equation cite98†1 over its reasoning and action tokens separately for each skill polarity. We compute turn-level Spearman correlations for $t<k_{i}$ (prefix), $t=k_{i}$ (diagnosed error), and $t>k_{i}$ (suffix).\nL391: The plot reports these token-mean gaps before the training coefficient $\\lambda$ is applied. Correlations are descriptive: multiple turns can share a prompt or anchor, and Figure cite76†1 does not report confidence intervals or post-filter coverage.\nL392: ### C.2 Controlled Recovery Protocol\nL393: To assess whether suffix truncation improves behavior after a recoverable error, we construct a controlled recovery suite from held-out WebShop tasks. We execute a pre-registered, plausible, recoverable wrong action from a shared reference trajectory, then start each actor from the same perturbed state and collect repeated continuations without hindsight skills.\nL394: The compared actors retain Positive prefix and Negative error-turn guidance during training but differ in suffix treatment: Suffix Positive continues Positive OPD, Suffix Negative continues Negative OPD, and ETRO retains only GRPO. We measure recovery success, normalized score, the proportion of first continuation actions classified by a blinded evaluator as wrong-branch continuation, and entropy over those first-turn action tokens.\nL395: The recovery results are reported in Table cite85†6 and discussed in Section cite24†4.4 .\nL396: \nL397: Experimental support, please cite99†view the build logs for errors. Generated by cite100†L A T E xml†math.nist.gov .\nL398: ## Instructions for reporting errors\nL399: \nL400: We are continuing to improve HTML versions of papers, and your feedback helps enhance accessibility and mobile support. To report errors in the HTML that will help us improve conversion and rendering, choose any of the methods listed below:\nL401: \nL402:   * Click the \"Report Issue\" () b